Found this while looking at how the report shortens long URLs. `Util.getURLDisplayName` tries to keep the file extension by slicing the name down to `MAX_LENGTH - 1 - extension.length` and then appending the extension. That subtraction goes negative whenever the text after the last dot is longer than 63 chars - which happens on any path like `/api.v2/<long-slug>`, where the last dot isn't really an extension at all. `String.slice(0, negative)` keeps almost everything, and the tail then gets appended on top, so the "shortened" name ends up longer than the original with the middle duplicated:

```
Util.parseURL('https://example.com/api.v2/this-is-a-very-long-endpoint-name-that-goes-on-and-on-forever').file
// before (133 chars, path repeated):
// /api.v2/this-is-a-very-long-endpoint-name-that-goes-on-and-on-forev….v2/this-is-a-very-long-endpoint-name-that-goes-on-and-on-forever
// after (64 chars, no repetition):
// /api.v2/this-is-a-very-long-endpoint-name-that-goes-on-and-on-f…
```

This is user visible: `report/renderer/details-renderer.js` `renderTextURL` uses `parsed.file` as the link text, so every `valueType: 'url'` table (network-requests, resource-summary, ...) shows that garbled string.

The change just guards the branch: if keeping the extension would leave nothing in front of it, fall back to the plain `slice(0, MAX_LENGTH - 1) + '…'` path that already existed. Behaviour for normal short extensions is unchanged.

Test: added a regression case in `core/test/lib/url-utils-test.js`. `run-mocha-tests.js --testMatch core/test/lib/url-utils-test.js` is 51 passing; reverting `shared/util.js` makes the new case fail. I also ran `core/test/lib/**` (494 passing, 1 failing) - the one failure is `asset-saver helper > elideLhrErrorStacks`, which fails the same way on unmodified main because of Windows path separators in stack traces, unrelated to this.
